{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c40d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a818-bded-7950-8bad-8c987713be85"},"sequence":97,"recorded_at":1780531400000189,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555"},"call_id":"call_01a0a818f8ae74f8ab7d8d5a779e675e","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/querying.md`.\n1|# Querying & Mutating the Board\n2|\n3|Three primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern, plus CLI for humans:\n4|\n5|## Concrete Assignment Default\n6|\n7|When a prompt already supplies a Task/Bug ID and task inputs, query only fields\n8|needed for the next decision:\n9|\n10|```bash\n11|task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status checklist outcomeResources }'\n12|task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\n13|```\n14|\n15|Do not add routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }` orientation.\n16|Use `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)` only after an unknown\n17|call. Board-wide observability and plans belong to an orchestrator routing\n18|decision; full reads remain explicit audit/debug surfaces.\n19|\n20|## DSL Queries — primary read approach for agents\n21|\n22|Compact queries that return structured output. Zero session overhead (no MCP handshake), batch multiple queries in a single call. **This is what agents should use for all structured reads.**\n23|\n24|**Output format:** Use `--format compact` for token-efficient output (~40% fewer tokens). Default is `--format json`.\n25|\n26|```bash\n27|# Board overview\n28|task-board q --format compact 'summary()'\n29|\n30|# Whole-project readiness with the existing summary folded in\n31|task-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\n32|\n33|# Scoped readiness at each board level\n34|task-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\n35|task-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260211-def) { observability-story }'\n36|task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { observability-task }'\n37|\n38|# Single element lookup\n39|task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status assignee }'\n40|\n41|# Bounded activity history (continue with cursor=<next_cursor>)\n42|task-board q --format compact \\\n43|  'activity(TASK-260211-abc, limit=25, order=descending, kinds=\"progress,content\", view=effective)'\n44|\n45|# Filtered listing with field preset\n46|task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'\n47|\n48|# Agent monitoring\n49|task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\n50|\n51|# Batch: multiple queries in one call (semicolon-separated)\n52|task-board q --format compact 'get(T1) { status }; get(T2) { status }; get(T3) { status }'\n53|\n54|# Zero-argument and parameterized operations can share a batch\n55|task-board q --format compact 'project_config(); models(agent=codex)'\n56|\n57|# Execution plan\n58|task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'\n59|\n60|# Schema introspection — discover available fields, presets, operations, and mutations\n61|task-board q 'schema()'\n62|task-board q 'schema(operation=project_config)'\n63|task-board q 'schema(mutation=set_status)'\n64|\n65|# Target-only spawn policy (explicit opt-in in every profile)\n66|task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'\n67|```\n68|\n69|**Operations:** `get(ID)`, `activity(ID, cursor?, limit?, order?, kinds?, view?)`, `list(filters...)`, `count(filters...)`, `summary()`, `plan(scope?, mode=?, active=?)`, `agents(stale=N?)`, `models(agent?)`, `project_config(view?, role?, agent?)`, `whoami()`, `schema(operation?|mutation?)`\n70|**Field projection:** `{ id status assignee }`; base presets: `minimal`, `default`, `overview`, `full`, `progress`, `planning`; observability presets: `observability-project`, `observability-epic`, `observability-story`, `observability-task`\n71|**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue. Element mutations in one batch run inside one activity transaction: when any statement is refused, the refused statements keep their own errors, every statement that would otherwise have succeeded is reported as `ok:false` with code `batch_rolled_back`, and nothing is persisted. A clean batch commits every statement and records one activity event per affected element, so a batch may change an element's checklist only once; issue further checklist changes as separate commands. Local and remote mutation commands both print that complete envelope before returning a nonzero exit, so the envelope always describes exactly what was persisted.\n72|**Format:** `--format compact` (agent default) or `--format json` (TUI/programmatic)\n73|\n74|**First-time setup:** Under the full profile, `schema()` returns the complete API contract. Under lite, skip the proactive full read and request only the failed/unknown call with `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)`. Parser errors for a known call include that same typed signature directly.\n75|\n76|`activity()` is deliberately separate from element projections: ordinary\n77|`get`/`list` reads never embed history. Its `limit` defaults to 50 and is bounded\n78|to 1..100. `order` accepts `ascending` or `descending`, `view` accepts `raw` or\n79|`effective`, and `kinds` accepts a comma-separated operation filter. Reuse only\n80|the opaque `next_cursor` returned by the previous page and keep the same order;\n81|the cursor is direction-bound and sequence-anchored for stable traversal across\n82|concurrent appends. The local CLI and remote `/q` endpoint share this contract.\n83|Activity is automatic infrastructure output; callers must not append routine\n84|history themselves. An explicit `legacy_pre_feature` boundary means older\n85|events are unavailable, not that they should be inferred. Deletion retains the\n86|terminal typed history. Operators correct a bad record only with confirmed,\n87|append-only `correct_activity`; the original remains in `view=raw`, while\n88|`view=effective` applies the correction, and remote correction requires Admin.\n89|Never replace bounded pages with `get(...){full}` or an unbounded filesystem\n90|read.\n91|\n92|`kinds` also selects the three DERIVED control-plane planes. `run` returns the\n93|spawn run lifecycle mirrored onto the run's task, `change_request` returns the\n94|Change Request transitions mirrored onto the element the CR is attached to, and\n95|`workspace` returns the Story workspace operations mirrored onto the Story:\n96|\n97|```bash\n98|task-board q --format compact 'activity(TASK-260211-abc, kinds=\"run,change_request\", limit=25)'\n99|task-board q --format compact 'activity(STORY-260211-abc, kinds=workspace, limit=25)'\n100|```\n101|\n102|These events carry one typed `ledger_event` change — domain, ledger id,\n103|enumerated event token, sequence, revision, from/to state tokens, a UTC\n104|instant, and typed `oid`/`ref`/`id`/`token` facts. They never carry a summary,\n105|message or reason: each ledger keeps its own human-readable text and the mirror\n106|cannot transport it. The default unfiltered view includes them alongside board\n107|mutations, and filtering by a board kind such as `kinds=progress` returns\n108|exactly what it returned before the planes existed.\n109|\n110|The runtime emits them automatically at the moment each ledger row is written.\n111|`record_ledger_event` exists so the local and remote transports share one\n112|statement and one validator, and it refuses any free-text payload.\n113|\n114|**It is not an agent-facing mutation.** `task-board m 'record_ledger_event(...)'`\n115|is refused, on a local and a remote board alike, with a\n116|`ledger_mirror_unauthorized` error: a mirror is DERIVED from a ledger row, so\n117|one appended by hand asserts a control-plane fact that never happened. On a\n118|local board the refusal is enforced twice over — once on the CLI mutation\n119|surface and once in the shared activity layer, which admits only the runtime's\n120|derived-mirror sink as the source entrypoint.\n121|\n122|**Stated bound — board-server boards.** The prevention above is a property of\n123|the CLI, not of the server. A mirror bound for a remote board is relayed over\n124|the same `m` transport, authenticating as the same principal, through the same\n125|handler entrypoint, as any other mutation that principal could send, so the\n126|server cannot tell a relayed mirror from one a write-capable principal composed\n127|by hand — and it admits both. What the remote plane holds instead is\n128|attribution: every mirror records the authenticated principal that sent it, so\n129|a hand-minted one is traceable to its author even though it is not prevented.\n130|Preventing it would need per-record attestation the transport has no field for;\n131|admin-gating the operation would break the ordinary agent's mirror, which is\n132|the behaviour the remote plane exists to provide.\n133|\n134|**Server capability is proven, never assumed.** Before sending a mirror to a\n135|board-server the CLI reads `/v1/version` and requires the `ledger_mirror`\n136|contract. A server that advertises none, or a different version, is refused\n137|with a message naming what it advertised — a server that cannot persist a\n138|mirror would refuse `record_ledger_event` as an unknown operation, which looks\n139|exactly like a mirror that was never attempted. A `/version` read that FAILS is\n140|refused too, and separately: a failed read is not an absence, and treating it\n141|as one would silently drop mirrors a capable server would have accepted.\n142|\n143|Run heartbeats are deliberately not mirrored — see\n144|`docs/activity-mutation-coverage.md` for that bound and for what\n145|`task-board validate` reports when a ledger row has no mirror.\n146|\n147|### Scoped signatures\n148|\n149|`schema(operation=NAME)` and `schema(mutation=NAME)` return one flat signature object: `kind`, `name`, a typed `signature`, description, parameters, and examples; mutations also report `destructive` and `idempotent`. They do not include unrelated fields, presets, operations, or mutation contracts. Provide exactly one scope. `schema()` without a scope retains the full legacy response.\n150|\n151|Known-operation parser failures preserve the underlying typed parse error and append the relevant signature. This lets an agent repair a call without reading the full schema.\n152|\n153|### Recovery examples from the Story binary\n154|\n155|Captured at Story commit `13afc672f01a38d5748cca533f33524aec8080d9`.\n156|From the repository root, build without installing:\n157|\n158|```bash\n159|(cd tools/board-cli && go build -o ../../.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board .)\n160|```\n161|\n162|The commands below use that binary and the authoritative board selected by\n163|`TASK_BOARD_DIR`. IDs refer to this Story's board; substitute existing IDs on\n164|another board. Output blocks quote the exact emitted text (errors are stderr).\n165|Exit 1 is an expected refusal, not a passing command.\n166|\n167|#### Unknown operation\n168|\n169|An unknown operation lists the known query operations and points to `schema()` for discovery and `schema(operation=NAME)` for one signature.\n170|\n171|```bash\n172|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'fetch()'\n173|```\n174|\n175|Output (exit 1):\n176|\n177|```text\n178|query error: parse error at 1:1: unknown operation \"fetch\" (got \"fetch\"); known operations: activity, agents, context, context_boundary, context_candidates, context_retention, contexts, count, get, list, models, plan, project_config, provider_limits, schema, summary, whoami; see schema() for the contract and schema(operation=NAME) for one signature\n179|```\n180|\n181|#### Statement grammar and bare positional arguments\n182|\n183|The scoped signature includes `syntax`: the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument, and subsequent arguments use `key=value`. Thus `get(TASK-260907-bg79el)` matches `get(id:string)`.\n184|\n185|```bash\n186|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'schema(operation=get)'\n187|```\n188|\n189|Output (exit 0):\n190|\n191|```text\n192|{\"description\":\"Get a single element by ID\",\"examples\":[\"get(TASK-XX) { overview }\"],\"kind\":\"operation\",\"name\":\"get\",\"parameters\":[{\"name\":\"id\",\"type\":\"string\",\"optional\":false,\"description\":\"Element ID\"}],\"signature\":\"get(id:string)\",\"syntax\":\"OPERATION(element-or-identifier, key=value, key=\\\"quoted string\\\") { field preset }; statements separated by ';'; the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument; remaining arguments use key=value (no operators or lists); string escapes: \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\ \\\\n \\\\t\"}\n193|```\n194|\n195|#### Unknown projection field\n196|\n197|A misspelled projection field suggests `updated` and points to `schema()` for valid fields and presets.\n198|\n199|```bash\n200|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'get(TASK-260907-bg79el) { updated_at }'\n201|```\n202|\n203|Output (exit 1):\n204|\n205|```text\n206|query error: parse error at 1:29: unknown field \"updated_at\" (got \"updated_at\"); did you mean `updated`?; see schema() for valid fields and preset names\n207|```\n208|\n209|#### Bare element ID\n210|\n211|An element ID alone is refused; wrap it in `get(ID) { fields }`.\n212|\n213|```bash\n214|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'TASK-260907-bg79el'\n215|```\n216|\n217|Output (exit 1):\n218|\n219|```text\n220|query error: an element ID is not an operation; use get(ID) { fields }\n221|```\n222|\n223|#### Keyword IDs on mutations\n224|\n225|Every id-first mutation accepts `id=...` (bare or quoted) as well as the first positional argument. This includes `link`, `add_checklist_item`, and `set_status`; supplying both forms is rejected. These successful dry runs preview without persisting changes.\n226|\n227|```bash\n228|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'link(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, blocked_by=TASK-260211-1saw6h)'\n229|```\n230|\n231|Output (exit 0):\n232|\n233|```text\n234|{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"blocked_by\":\"TASK-260211-1saw6h\",\"dry_run\":true,\"id\":\"TASK-260907-bg79el\"}}\n235|```\n236|\n237|```bash\n238|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'add_checklist_item(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, text=\"Verify recovery guidance\")'\n239|```\n240|\n241|Output (exit 0):\n242|\n243|```text\n244|{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"dry_run\":true,\"id\":\"TASK-260907-bg79el\",\"text\":\"Verify recovery guidance\"}}\n245|```\n246|\n247|```bash\n248|.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'set_status(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, status=development)'\n249|```\n250|\n251|Output (exit 0):\n252|\n253|```text\n254|{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"dry_run\":true,\"id\":\"TASK-260907-bg79el\",\"status\":\"development\"}}\n255|```\n256|\n257|### `project_config()` runtime shape\n258|\n259|`project_config()` is always resolved by the local CLI, including when normal\n260|board reads and writes target a remote server. Its current top-level keys are\n261|`configured_mode`, `effective_mode`, `board_source`,\n262|`explicit_remote_override`, `project_root`, `config_path`, `config_exists`,\n263|`local`, `remote`, `whoami`, `goals`, `spawn`, `codex`, and `version_control`.\n264|\n265|The no-argument shape remains the role-optional general config surface in every\n266|profile, including the full default. For a spawn decision, opt in explicitly:\n267|\n268|```bash\n269|task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'\n270|```\n271|\n272|`role` is required. `agent` is optional and otherwise follows the same\n273|preferred-provider/schedule/runtime-affinity resolver as `task-board spawn`.\n274|The narrow result contains `enabled`, `max_parallel`, a small effective\n275|agent-context profile/source header, the effective allowed and target providers,\n276|the active schedule window, canonical role, merged role ceiling for the target\n277|provider, provider selection mode, and launch-composition enabled/status. It\n278|excludes local/remote auth, `whoami`, Codex session, nested agent-context policy,\n279|project paths, all unrelated provider ceilings, and version-control fields.\n280|Active schedule allow-sets and provider refusals use the same fail-closed\n281|resolver as spawn.\n282|\n283|For Codex, `resolved_role_ceiling` includes the resolved boolean `fast_mode`,\n284|`fast_mode_source` (`default` or `configured`), and `provenance.fast_mode` with\n285|the exact root or remote key. Durable spawn status and JSON run state carry\n286|`service_tier`, `fast_mode`, `fast_mode_source`, and `fast_mode_config_key`;\n287|provider-limit retries update them to describe the provider currently being\n288|launched, while each `provider_attempts[]` entry preserves the tier evidence\n289|carried by that historical launch.\n290|\n291|The lite profile's resolved `queries.project_config=spawn-preflight` setting\n292|guides agents toward the target-specific call; it does not rewrite a bare\n293|`project_config()` invocation. Supplying a view or target argument opts into\n294|the narrow projection, whose role remains mandatory.\n295|\n296|The policy members relevant to spawning and status mutation are:\n297|\n298|| Path | Shape |\n299||---|---|\n300|| `spawn.preferred_agent` | Legacy configured provider string, or `null`. It is retained only as compatibility evidence. |\n301|| `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` | Currently effective `{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}`, or `null`. During an active schedule window, `source` is `schedule`. |\n302|| `spawn.schedule` | `{configured, timezone, config_key, windows, active_window, base_preferred_agentic_system}`. `timezone` is always `UTC`; every projected window also carries its normalized bounds, key, and provider-system projection. |\n303|| `spawn.launch_composition` | `{enabled, config_key, contract, schema_version, degradation}` without invoking `agents-infra`. |\n304|| `spawn.ceilings` | Always reports effective `contract_version`, `authority`, `configured`, and `config_path`. V3 providers expose normalized `allowed_models` or `entries`, nullable `resolution_model`, confirmation mode, effort direction, merged role-effective policy, and exact provenance. Every contract version and input form reports `admitted_pairs`, the canonical admitted `(model, effort)` set with its digest — the field to read when deciding what a policy admits, since `allowed_models` is `null` under the entries form. An entries policy also reports `entries_expanded`, `entries_expansion_pinned`, and any unreachable/redundant entry `warnings`. V2 exposes `snapshot_version`, `snapshot_allowed_models`, `migration_required`, stable actionable warnings, and exact read-only v3 `migration` objects. |\n305|| `spawn.selection` | Required flags plus provider effort vocabularies and supported model criteria. |\n306|| `spawn.execution` / `spawn.tracking` | Executable background mode and durable run-observation contract. |\n307|| `goals` | Static compact `board-goal-v2` capability with required Session Manager native reflection and the one-release `primary-goal-v1` / `board-goal-v1` adapters. The legacy `goals.primary` descriptor remains nested alongside the v2 fields for the compatibility release. It never embeds the current objective and is omitted from `view=spawn-preflight`. |\n308|| `version_control` | `{configured, confirm, desired_commit_time, enforced_types, owner_message, acknowledgement_parameter, acknowledgement_value, config_key}`. |\n309|\n310|Local mode uses `spawn.*` keys. Remote mode uses `remote.spawn.*` when that\n311|object is present and non-null; otherwise it falls back to the whole root\n312|`spawn` object. The reported `spawn.config_key_prefix` and nested `config_key`\n313|values always identify the effective source. `preferred_agentic_system` is the\n314|canonical config spelling; `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.\n315|\n316|`spawn-policy-v3` admission is exact membership in the effective\n317|`allowed_models` set. `PolicyRank`, lifecycle, recommendation, registry order,\n318|and price are not policy inputs. A v2 projection is compatibility evidence,\n319|not an automatic migration: setup, query, spawn, and install never rewrite the\n320|repository. For an explicit migration, copy the full projection's provider and\n321|role `migration` objects under `contract_version: spawn-policy-v3`, review the\n322|diff, then repeat full and target-role preflight queries. An effort-only v2\n323|warning uses reason `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` and\n324|explicitly says to omit `resolution_model` unless a fallback is intended.\n325|\n326|### Project observability preset family\n327|\n328|`summary() { observability-project }` returns the whole-project view. The\n329|element presets work with the existing `get` and `list` operations; the task\n330|preset also applies to Bugs because Tasks and Bugs share the leaf estimate\n331|contract.\n332|\n333|Every preset exposes the same nested `observability` field:\n334|\n335|- `scope` identifies the project or selected Epic/Story/Task/Bug.\n336|- `summary` carries the existing `byType`, `active`, and `blocked` summary,\n337|  scoped to that element and its descendants.\n338|- `readiness.estimateKind` carries the leaf or calculated\n339|  Story/Epic/project projection state without dumping every missing leaf.\n340|- `readiness.coverage` always reports `estimated`, `unestimated`, `total`,\n341|  `ratio`, and `complete`, including fully estimated scopes.\n342|- `readiness.rolledEstimate` is the known Fibonacci sum even when coverage is\n343|  partial. Query the separate `estimate` field on the incomplete Story/Epic to\n344|  retrieve its exact `unestimatedTaskIds` drill-down list.\n345|- `isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard dependency state across the\n346|  scope, while `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` aggregate\n347|  derived cross-container waiting. The nested summary retains blocker details.\n348|- Only the project preset adds `primary_goal`. Its active form is\n349|  `{active:true,id,revision,objective,updated_at}`; after a clear or before\n350|  any creation it is `{active:false,latest_ref}` (where `latest_ref` can be\n351|  `null`). Epic, Story, Task, and Bug observability deliberately do not repeat\n352|  this board-wide parent intent or treat it as element acceptance.\n353|\n354|Plain `summary()` keeps its legacy response shape for compatibility.\n355|\n356|## DSL Mutations — structured writes for agents\n357|\n358|Write operations through the same DSL engine. All mutations support `--dry-run` preview. Destructive mutations require `--confirm`.\n359|\n360|Element-ID mutations accept the first positional argument or `id=ID` (bare or quoted), matching their schema signatures: `link(ID, blocked_by=OTHER)` and `link(id=\"ID\", blocked_by=OTHER)` are equivalent. Supplying both forms is rejected. `create(type=..., parent=...)` retains its keyword syntax.\n361|\n362|```bash\n363|# Status changes\n364|task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=development)'\n365|\n366|# Evidence-backed role handoff (preferred producer end transition)\n367|task-board handoff TASK-12 --role developer\n368|\n369|# Task/Bug estimate enum\n370|task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=notEstimated)'\n371|task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'\n372|\n373|# Create elements\n374|task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task)'\n375|task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task, description=\"Implement feature\", scope=\"Backend only\", ac=\"Tests pass\")'\n376|\n377|# Update README fields\n378|task-board m 'set_details(TASK-12, title=\"New title\", description=\"Updated desc\")'\n379|\n380|# Rename overview name/slug while preserving ID, README, progress, children, and resources\n381|task-board m 'rename(TASK-12, name=better-task-name)'\n382|\n383|# Assignment\n384|task-board m 'assign(TASK-12, agent=claude-opus)'\n385|task-board m 'unassign(TASK-12)'\n386|\n387|# Dependencies\n388|task-board m 'link(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'\n389|task-board m 'unlink(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'\n390|\n391|# Checklist\n392|task-board m 'check_item(TASK-12, item=1)'\n393|task-board m 'uncheck_item(TASK-12, item=1)'\n394|task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-12, text=\"Write tests\")'\n395|\n396|# Notes\n397|task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text=\"Progress update\")'        # append\n398|task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text=\"Replace all\", set=true)'  # replace\n399|\n400|# Move to new parent\n401|task-board m 'set_parent(TASK-12, parent=STORY-03)'\n402|\n403|# Resources\n404|task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-12, name=\"TASK-12_design.md\", type=precondition, content=\"...\", description=\"Design doc\")'\n405|task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-12, name=\"TASK-12_design.md\", type=precondition, content=\"revised\", description=\"Design doc v2\")'\n406|task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-12, name=\"TASK-12_design.md\")'\n407|\n408|# Destructive — preview first, then confirm\n409|task-board m --dry-run 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'\n410|task-board m --confirm 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'\n411|task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-12)'\n412|task-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-12)'\n413|\n414|# Batch mutations (semicolon-separated)\n415|task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=done); set_status(TASK-13, status=development)'\n416|```\n417|\n418|**Safety flags:**\n419|- `--dry-run` — preview mutation without applying. Returns what would change\n420|- `--confirm` — required for destructive mutations (`delete`, `delete_resource`)\n421|- Mutation arguments are schema-checked. Unknown named arguments and extra positional fragments are rejected with typed errors before any write runs.\n422|- Without `--confirm`, destructive mutations are rejected with an error\n423|\n424|**Format:** `--format json` (default) or `--format compact`\n425|\n426|**Introspection:** Run `task-board q 'schema(mutation=NAME)'` for one mutation signature, or full `schema()` for the entire contract.\n427|\n428|`task-board handoff` resolves the role's configured end status, verifies all\n429|checklist items plus an actual stored task-scoped outcome, and serializes that\n430|decision with the ordinary status transition. The underlying shared `handoff`\n431|mutation carries the already-resolved role/status for remote execution; use the\n432|CLI facade so callers do not have to reproduce role-policy resolution.\n433|\n434|The `estimate` q field is a nested enum. Task/Bug values are either\n435|`{\"kind\":\"notEstimated\"}` or\n436|`{\"kind\":\"estimated\",\"scale\":{\"kind\":\"fibonacci\",\"number\":5}}`.\n437|Story/Epic values are calculated, never persisted or hand-set. Full coverage\n438|returns the same `estimated` shape with the descendant sum. Partial coverage\n439|returns\n440|`{\"kind\":\"partiallyEstimated\",\"rolledEstimate\":{\"kind\":\"estimated\",\"scale\":{\"kind\":\"fibonacci\",\"number\":5}},\"notEstimatedCount\":\"1/3\",\"unestimatedTaskIds\":[\"TASK-...\"]}`.\n441|Story counts direct Task/Bug children; Epic rolls up descendant Task/Bug values.\n442|New Task/Bug records must be estimated before entering `development`; legacy\n443|records with no persisted Estimate section remain compatible and count as\n444|unestimated in container projections.\n445|\n446|## Grep — unstructured text search\n447|\n448|When DSL can't help — searching for arbitrary text across board files.\n449|\n450|```bash\n451|# Find mentions of a term\n452|task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"\n453|\n454|# Search in specific file type\n455|task-board grep \"blocked.*deployment\" --file progress.md\n456|\n457|# Case-insensitive search in descriptions\n458|task-board grep \"TODO\" -i --file README.md\n459|\n460|# With context lines\n461|task-board grep \"error\" -C 2\n462|```\n463|\n464|## CLI — human-facing output and complex operations\n465|\n466|CLI text output with ANSI colors is designed for humans. Use for operations not covered by DSL, or when human-readable output is needed.\n467|\n468|When reporting board state to a person, reference each epic, story, task, or bug with both its concrete ID and its human-readable title/name. In practice, that usually means carrying both `id` and `name` in your query output, or using a preset such as `default` or `overview` that already includes them. ID-only references are not enough for chat summaries because readers should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n469|\n470|```bash\n471|# Good human-facing summary inputs\n472|task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { id name status }'\n473|task-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'\n474|```\n475|\n476|### Parent alignment in the unified goal ledger\n477|\n478|Use the top-level `goal` group to maintain one provider-neutral, board-scoped\n479|statement of the current `parent/primary` owner's complete objective. It\n480|survives a parent-session replacement, records immutable `board-goal-v2`\n481|revisions, and never changes a board element's status or acceptance evidence.\n482|The command group is the one-release `primary-goal-v1` compatibility adapter.\n483|\n484|```bash\n485|# Create only when no primary goal is active.\n486|task-board goal set-primary \\\n487|  --objective \"Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements.\" \\\n488|  --reason \"First actionable requirement\"\n489|\n490|# CAS-replace the whole objective. Repeating the same objective is idempotent.\n491|task-board goal update --if-revision 1 \\\n492|  --objective \"Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work.\" \\\n493|  --reason \"Requirement expanded\"\n494|\n495|# Read current state, immutable audit history, or one immutable revision.\n496|task-board goal get\n497|task-board goal get --history\n498|task-board goal get --goal-id PRIMARY-GOAL-260723-a1b2c3 --revision 1\n499|\n500|# Clear only with the observed revision and an explicit disposition reason.\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
