{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c583","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a818-bded-7950-8bad-8c987713be85"},"sequence":284,"recorded_at":1780531400000563,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"3d0e694b-dcbb-457c-8e66-a4082a9a6555"},"call_id":"call_01a0a81975a97543972964de52c80a4f","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md`.\n1|# Statuses\n2|\n3|10 statuses organized in a workflow:\n4|\n5|| Status | Meaning | Who sets |\n6||--------|---------|----------|\n7|| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |\n8|| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |\n9|| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |\n10|| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |\n11|| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |\n12|| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |\n13|| **integrating** | Accepted Change Request, not landed. Active, never terminal | `accept_cr` only; left only by the integration transaction |\n14|| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | integration transaction for Change Request work; reviewer verdict plus the acknowledged transition for inline work; parent aggregation |\n15|| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |\n16|| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |\n17|\n18|## Status Flow\n19|\n20|**Happy path:**\n21|```\n22|backlog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → integrating → done\n23|```\n24|\n25|`integrating` is on the path for work a Change Request owns — the repository\n26|delta produced in a managed Story workspace. `accept_cr` is the only way in and\n27|the integration transaction is the only way out; see step 4a below. Work done\n28|inline, with no Change Request, has no `integrating` step and goes\n29|`reviewing → done` on the reviewer's accepted verdict, with the commit\n30|acknowledgement when `version_control.confirm` is on.\n31|\n32|**Returns from reviewing:**\n33|- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research\n34|- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework\n35|\n36|**Terminal from any status:**\n37|- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled\n38|- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision\n39|\n40|**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved. `handoff` refuses terminal elements; `set_status` refuses leaving `done` or `closed` with `TerminalStatusError` (`terminal_status`), naming the current terminal state. An identical `set_status` is idempotent.\n41|\n42|For a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`\n43|means it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be\n44|closed from any lifecycle state.\n45|\n46|For a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is\n47|`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the\n48|container itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and\n49|transitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also\n50|satisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close\n51|containers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports\n52|`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active\n53|descendants.\n54|\n55|Validation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic\n56|stored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local\n57|board may explicitly set\n58|`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to\n59|`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active\n60|containers and every container with one or more children remain strict.\n61|\n62|**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.\n63|\n64|**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:\n65|1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.\n66|2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\n67|3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.\n68|4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.\n69|4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation writes `accepted` and `integrating` atomically: it sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, binds the revision to its **producer role and archetype**, and routes the element to `integrating` — accepted but not landed, and accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator routes a new tracked run carrying that exact producer role and archetype; that run — not the Orchestrator — runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and leaves the leaf `integrating`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` creates no commit and likewise stays `integrating`. `done` is produced only by the integration transaction, and `worktree checkpoint` or `worktree integrate` from a reviewer, an orchestrator, or any run whose role/archetype is not the bound producer's is refused. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.\n70|\n71|4b. **`checkpoint` never closes anything.** A checkpoint makes no board transition at all: the leaf it commits stays `integrating`. When the leaf is the Story's last open child it is also its Story's integration unit, so `worktree checkpoint` refuses it with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch and is the only thing that makes the `done` transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.\n72|\n73|A checkpointed leaf is closed by its Story's integration, not by a later status change. `worktree integrate` lands the Story branch, which carries every checkpoint commit on it, so the same transaction that makes the final leaf `done` also closes every sibling that is `integrating` with durable checkpoint evidence — and only those. A sibling accepted but never checkpointed has nothing on the branch to land: it keeps blocking, and the fix is to checkpoint it, not to close it. That is also why a checkpointed sibling does not hide the Story's final leaf: the leaf that would close the scope is computed against the leaves this landing will carry.\n74|5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.\n75|6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.\n76|7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.\n77|\n78|`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.\n79|\n80|`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes \"the reviewer decided nothing\" from \"the reviewer decided\", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.\n81|\n82|`to-review` therefore means one thing: a producer handoff awaiting review. An accepted Change Request is `integrating`, never `to-review`; `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say how far that integration has come.\n83|\n84|A tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`\n85|for its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to\n86|run `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and\n87|outcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.\n88|\n89|`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.\n90|\n91|Human-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\"; it is not \"done\", \"complete\", \"finished\", \"final\", or \"готово\". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.\n92|\n93|## Per-Element Review Policy\n94|\n95|Every board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with\n96|`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with\n97|`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query\n98|projection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to\n99|`required`.\n100|\n101|- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an\n102|  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n103|- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.\n104|- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`\n105|  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,\n106|  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the\n107|  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n108|\n109|This policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and\n110|never introduces routine human review.\n111|\n112|## blocked vs is_blocked\n113|\n114|Two different concepts:\n115|\n116|- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.\n117|- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked\n118|\n119|CLI display:\n120|```\n121|TASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency\n122|TASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status\n123|```\n124|\n125|## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation\n126|\n127|After a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct\n128|children. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an\n129|assigned child without implying ownership of the container.\n130|\n131|- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.\n132|  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do\n133|  not silently normalize it to `done`.\n134|- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:\n135|  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.\n136|  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked\n137|  children keeps its current manual status.\n138|- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then\n139|  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not\n140|  recomputed.\n141|\n142|```bash\n143|task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'\n144|# TASK-260205-abc123 → development\n145|# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)\n146|# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)\n147|```\n148|\n149|For Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into\n150|`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with\n151|`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal\n152|is a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start\n153|refusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are\n154|grandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for\n155|an element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.\n156|\n157|### Commit confirmation for accepted work\n158|\n159|This gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope\n160|as atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is\n161|committed — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other\n162|commit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to\n163|split commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover\n164|decides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or\n165|feature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,\n166|backdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human\n167|authorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit\n168|approval.\n169|\n170|This section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn\n171|templates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back\n172|here; they must not restate the mechanics below.\n173|\n174|Top-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to\n175|`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation\n176|that would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local\n177|acknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story\n178|transition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its\n179|parent Story.\n180|\n181|```json\n182|{\n183|  \"version_control\": {\n184|    \"confirm\": true,\n185|    \"desired_commit_time\": \"evening MSK\"\n186|  }\n187|}\n188|```\n189|\n190|```bash\n191|task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'\n192|```\n193|\n194|The error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story\n195|transition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The\n196|acknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is\n197|allowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge\n198|`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely\n199|uncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and\n200|status writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first\n201|requires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then\n202|the server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses\n203|under one serialized mutation.\n204|\n205|A reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`, and must not\n206|escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human. It records acceptance\n207|evidence and hands it to the producer side — never to the human; every `done`\n208|transition belongs there.\n209|Which producer depends on the flow. An element under a Change Request routes\n210|through `accept_cr` to `integrating` and is closed by the integration\n211|transaction of step 4a, which supplies the acknowledgement itself. An inline\n212|element with no Change Request keeps this gate: the producer that owns the\n213|committed scope commits it and then makes the acknowledged transition.\n214|\n215|Board structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the\n216|rule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in\n217|[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).\n218|\n219|Structural reactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in\n220|`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status\n221|runs ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. A terminal descendant cannot\n222|be reactivated with `set_status` or `handoff`; create new work instead. Any `done` or `closed`\n223|container on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;\n224|explicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target\n225|is the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:\n226|\n227|```bash\n228|task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'\n229|# Precondition: TASK-260205-abc123 is nonterminal.\n230|# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev\n231|# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n232|# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)\n233|```\n234|\n235|Automatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies\n236|still gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and\n237|`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left\n238|unchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the\n239|same dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `development`\n240|and `to-review` — the two transitions that assert a specific worker is doing or\n241|has done the work. `to-dev` is deliberately exempt: it asserts that the leaf is\n242|queued and owned by nobody, so requiring an owner in order to record that would\n243|be self-contradictory and would leave an unassigned blocked leaf with no route\n244|back onto the developer queue.\n245|\n246|`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do\n247|not select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being\n248|silently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container\n249|that still has a blocked descendant. Resolve or close that descendant work; a direct terminal-status exit is refused.\n250|For historical post-landing regressions, see the proven-landing\n251|[`restore-done` route](tracked-background-spawn.md#integration-run-termination-and-historical-status-recovery).\n252|\n253|## Story Activation Reminders\n254|\n255|An `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production\n256|mutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story\n257|transitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never\n258|fetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and\n259|never replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in\n260|[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —\n261|a reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still\n262|succeeds.\n263|\n264|### Trigger\n265|\n266|A change qualifies when all of the following hold:\n267|\n268|- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).\n269|- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly\n270|  `backlog`.\n271|- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other\n272|  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a\n273|  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a\n274|  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old\n275|  value).\n276|\n277|Both the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a\n278|Task/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`\n279|still emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never\n280|qualifies on its own ID prefix.\n281|\n282|### Deduplication and order\n283|\n284|Within one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side\n285|effects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story\n286|ID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice\n287|(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still\n288|emits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get\n289|their own reminder, in first-seen order.\n290|\n291|### Payload shape\n292|\n293|```json\n294|{\n295|  \"code\": \"story_worktree_base_preflight_required\",\n296|  \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z\",\n297|  \"message\": \"Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.\",\n298|  \"story_id\": \"STORY-260218-bbbbbb\",\n299|  \"trunk_ref\": \"refs/heads/main\",\n300|  \"trunk_oid\": \"8c3f1a9...\",\n301|  \"upstream_ref\": \"refs/remotes/protected/main\",\n302|  \"upstream_oid\": \"5e2b7d0...\",\n303|  \"selected_base_oid\": null\n304|}\n305|```\n306|\n307|`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every\n308|reminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).\n309|`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and\n310|`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,\n311|never omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The\n312|`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one\n313|change qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all\n314|(not an empty array).\n315|\n316|### Authority evidence: local versus remote\n317|\n318|The five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after\n319|the mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:\n320|\n321|- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior\n322|  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded\n323|  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/\n324|  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the\n325|  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it\n326|  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the\n327|  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the\n328|  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever\n329|  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because\n330|  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure\n331|  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`\n332|  rather than failing the mutation.\n333|- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared\n334|  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the\n335|  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git\n336|  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only\n337|  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.\n338|\n339|### Mutation coverage\n340|\n341|`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of\n342|production mutations route their persisted changes through:\n343|\n344|| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |\n345|| --- | --- |\n346|| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |\n347|| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |\n348|| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |\n349|| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |\n350|| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |\n351|| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |\n352|\n353|`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because\n354|they share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never\n355|produce a qualifying change themselves. The \"not wired\" column takes no `cfg`\n356|at all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller\n357|crafted a matching change shape.\n358|\n359|## Landing a Story on trunk\n360|\n361|This section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path\n362|where a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:\n363|`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed\n364|Story workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in\n365|[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);\n366|what belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.\n367|\n368|**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with\n369|`accept_cr`, which routes the element to `integrating` and binds the revision to\n370|its producer role and archetype. No Git operation has happened at that point and\n371|nothing is `done`. The Orchestrator then routes a new tracked run with that\n372|producer binding; that run integrates, and the `done` transition is the *last*\n373|board effect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the\n374|board first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an\n375|acknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.\n376|\n377|```bash\n378|task-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \\\n379|  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \\\n380|  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00\n381|```\n382|\n383|**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the\n384|`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no\n385|suite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project\n386|with nothing to run states that explicitly as `{\"commands\": []}`. See\n387|[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).\n388|\n389|**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story\n390|candidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task\n391|checkpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever\n392|fast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is\n393|required, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the\n394|committer date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this\n395|repository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,\n396|that is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With\n397|confirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.\n398|\n399|**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command\n400|supplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that\n401|transition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely\n402|contained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded\n403|candidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is\n404|exactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than\n405|a clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected\n406|state at the moment the acknowledgement is written:\n407|\n408|- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit\n409|  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what\n410|  produces the board bytes it records.\n411|- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an\n412|  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its\n413|  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else\n414|  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's\n415|  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state\n416|  that this command neither examines nor stages.\n417|- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above\n418|  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.\n419|\n420|**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted\n421|candidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit\n422|titled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no\n423|repository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —\n424|its board record is the whole of its work product.\n425|\n426|**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable\n427|transaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the\n428|recorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is\n429|a no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one\n430|phase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with\n431|`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial\n432|integration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
